## 3. Audit conclusion

This is the first Zero Trust Supplier Payment / Shipment Release
business-semantic sandbox WOW runner. It is not production E2E. It is not real
supplier API. It is not real bank API. It is not real warehouse API. It does
not execute payment. It does not release shipment. It does not call
network/Gemini/live model/connectors/secrets. It preserves Root final
authority.

## Sandbox Fixture Boundary

- The runner uses deterministic local fixtures.
- Fake evidence models warehouse stock, purchase order, supplier invoice,
  supplier provenance, legal/compliance document, bank/payment slot, stale DRS
  memory, conflicting supplier record, mock human approval, and mock receipt.
- Local fixtures preserve closed-layer boundary semantics.
- Local fixtures do not prove production connectors.
- Local fixtures do not prove real external API behavior.
- Local fixtures do not create real action permission.
- This runner is business-semantic proof, not production integration.

## Existing Closed Layer Semantics Reused

- Real Local DRS Resolver / Writeback semantics are represented through local
  DRS write/resolve candidate context.
- CandidateVectorGenerator + AVF semantics are represented through candidate
  vector / AVF score/ranking counters.
- AVF Candidate Advisory / GT-LGT Advisory semantics are represented through
  candidate advisory review.
- Bounded LLM/SLM Actors semantics are represented through bounded actor route
  that cannot command bank or supplier.
- Fractal Cell Runtime Integration semantics are represented through child
  branch reports returning upward.
- Post V&V / GT / Root boundary semantics are represented through downstream
  review and Root final business summary.
- The runner does not claim to call every underlying production API directly.
- The runner does not bypass Root.

